/-!
# Geometric branches and irreducible components

The number of (geometric) branches of a scheme at a point is the sum of the numbers of
(geometric) branches of its irreducible components through that point, each taken with its
reduced induced scheme structure. Both readings of the parenthetical are proved: the
geometric count, whose branches are the minimal primes of a strict henselization, and the
ordinary count, whose branches are the minimal primes of a henselization.

The proof is in two halves. On the ring side, a strict henselization is faithfully flat
over the local ring, so its minimal primes fibre over the minimal primes of the base and
the fibre over a minimal prime `p` is the set of minimal primes of the quotient of the
strict henselization by the ideal generated by `p`; that quotient is itself a strict
henselization of the quotient of the base. On the geometric side, the irreducible
components of the scheme through the point correspond to the minimal primes of its local
ring, an identification that is transported from an affine chart through the open
immersion, and the local ring of a reduced irreducible component at the point is the
quotient of the local ring by the corresponding minimal prime.

## Main results

* `AlgebraicGeometry.numberOfGeometricBranches_eq_iSup_sum_components` expresses the
  geometric branch count at a point as a sum over the irreducible components through it.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.numberOfBranches_eq_iSup_sum_components` is the same statement for the
  ordinary branch count, which is the other reading of the parenthetical "(geometric)" in the
  source. Its proof is the same argument with the henselization in place of the strict
  henselization.
* `isGeometricallyUnibranchRing_of_ringEquiv` transports geometric unibranchness of a local
  ring along a ring isomorphism.

## Implementation notes

The reduced induced structure on a component is given by its vanishing ideal sheaf. The
point of this subscheme is the point of the support corresponding to the original point.
The possibly infinite sum in `ℕ∞` is written as the supremum of its finite partial sums
over finite sets of components.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 0E20](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0E20)
-/

@[expose] public noncomputable section

open TopologicalSpace CategoryTheory CategoryTheory.Limits
